%[text] **2. Stability** (20 marks)
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:7f59)
%[text] Consider the system shown above where:
%[text] $D(s)=\\frac{K(s+2)}{s}\\;\\mbox{ and } \\;G(s)=\\frac{s+1}{s^3+8s^2-18s+20}$
%[text] a) (5 marks) Use Matlab's symbolic computing to compute the closed loop transfer function of the system. Assign the computed closed loop transfer function the variable $\\texttt{G\\\_cl\\\_symQ2a}$.
clear
% You code goes here
syms s K

% Define symbolic plant G(s)
G_sym = (s + 1) / (s^3 + 8*s^2 - 18*s + 20);

% Define symbolic controller D(s)
D_sym = K * (s + 2) / s;

% Open-loop transfer function L(s)
L_sym = D_sym * G_sym;

% Closed-loop transfer function G_cl(s) = L(s) / (1 + L(s))
G_cl_symQ2a = simplify( L_sym / (1 + L_sym) ) %[output:602a2d20]
%[text] b) (5 marks) For what values of K is the closed loop system stable? ? Justify your answer using matlab and explanation.
% Your code goes here
s = tf('s');

% Plant and controller (same as 2a, but using a placeholder value for K, which is K = 1, to sketch root locus for K > 0)
G = (s + 1) / (s^3 + 8*s^2 - 18*s + 20);

D = (s + 2) / s;

% Open-loop transfer function L(s) = D(s)G(s)
L = D * G;

% Check open-loop poles first
disp('Open-loop poles (K=0):') %[output:93baf727]
pole(L) %[output:14293ce3]
% Find stable K range
K_vec = linspace(0, 100, 100000); 
[P, K_vec] = rlocus(L,K_vec);
stable = find(all(real(P) < 0, 1));

if isempty(stable) %[output:group:3b8b422a]
    disp('Never stable')
else
    idx = stable(1);                % first K where ALL poles are stable
    K_crit = K_vec(idx)             % critical gain %[output:42f249b1]
    P_crit = P(:, idx)              % poles at critical gain %[output:588995ee]
    fprintf('Stable for K > %.4f\n', K_crit) %[output:4ac70df8]
end %[output:group:3b8b422a]
%[text] For any K greater than ~40.1174, the system is stable. For any K between 0 and 40.1174 causes the increase the amplitude without any bound, making the system unstable.
%[text] c) (5 marks) For the value of $K$ when the system is marginally stable (i.e. the closed loop system has pole(s) of multiplicity 1 on the $j\\omega$-axis and the other closed loop poles are in the open leftt half plane - from your answer to (2b) you know the value of $K$ where this occurs), calculate the value of the poles on the$j\\omega$-axis. Assign the values to the variable $\\texttt{omega\\\_crossingQ2c}$ . 
% Extract only the imaginary axis poles (real part ≈ 0)
omega_crossingQ2c = P_crit(abs(real(P_crit)) < 0.1) %[output:46bfac19]
%[text] d) (5 marks) When the closed loop system is marginally stable, it is not Bounded Input Bounded Output (BIBO) stable because there are bounded inputs that cause an unbounded output. Give a bounded input $r(t)$ that would cause an unbounded output when the closed loop system is marginally stable.
% Your code goes here
syms t
% Force real value by taking only real part
omega_crossingQ2c_val = abs(imag(omega_crossingQ2c(1))); % extract from imaginary pole

r(t) = sin(omega_crossingQ2c_val * t);
bounded_input=vpa(r(t), 4) %[output:97470759]
