      0   0   1    0; %[output:400f526e]
      0   0   0    1; %[output:400f526e]
      0  -10 -17  -8 ] %[output:group:2187e21b] %[output:400f526e]
B = [0; 0; 0; 1] %[output:8fd7d89c]
C = [-16 0 1 0] %[output:66361aa5]
D = 0 %[output:5f67b7b9]
% Verify transfer function matches
sys = ss(A, B, C, D);
G_check = tf(sys) %[output:8219d9b9]

%[text] b) (5 marks) You want to design state-variable feedback controller that has 20.8% overshoot and settling time of 4 seconds for plant.
%[text] What are the desired locations of the 2 dominant closed loop poles of the system?
% Your code goes here
% Desired specs
PO = 0.208;      % 20.8% overshoot
Ts = 4;         % 4 s settling time 

% Solve for damping ratio zeta from PO formula
zeta = -log(PO) / sqrt(pi^2 + (log(PO))^2);

% Natural frequency from Ts ≈ 4/(zeta*wn)
wn = 4/(zeta*Ts);

% Desired dominant poles
sigma = -zeta*wn;
wd    = wn*sqrt(1 - zeta^2) %[output:8fb651b0]
p1 = sigma + 1j*wd;
p2 = sigma - 1j*wd;

p1, p2 %[output:17f01ef7] %[output:776e4319]
%[text] **Where would you place the other two poles? Why.**
%[text] Place the remaining two poles significantly farther left on the real axis than the dominant pair, e.g.:
%[text] - s\_3= -5
%[text] - s\_4= -6 \
%[text] Reason:
%[text] Poles far to the left (at least 5 time farther left than the dominant pair) have much faster dynamics (large negative real part), so their modes decay quickly and do not significantly affect the transient response. This keeps the closed‑loop behavior effectively governed by the designed dominant pair at -1 + j\*2.0007 and -1 - j\*2.0007, preserving the desired 20.8% overshoot and 4 s settling time.
%[text] 
